+## Events, captures, enroll, match, delete
+
+All from the finger-on-sensor trace of the Windows driver, implemented in
+the driver but not yet run against a finger (see the test notes at the end).
+
+### Events
+
+The interrupt endpoint 0x83 sends 7 byte notices (byte 6 is a running
+number, nothing else is needed). What happened is read with EVENT_READ.
+
+- `86 <mask u32> 0*12 <mask u32> 0*12 <flag u32>` EVENT_CONFIG, 37 bytes.
+  The mask is repeated at offset 1 and 17. Used: 0x06 (finger down and up),
+  0x04 (finger up), 0x01000000 (frame acquired, event 24) and mask 0 with
+  flag 4 to turn everything off. The reply is 64 bytes, the last u16 (offset
+  62 of the payload) is the number of the next event.
+- `87 <seq u16> 20 00 01 00 00 00` EVENT_READ. Payload: u32 count, then
+  12 byte events: u16 type, u16 number, u32 timestamp, u32 flags. Types:
+  1 finger down, 2 finger up, 24 frame acquired. Flags bit 31 on a frame
+  event means the capture was rejected (the Windows log says "reject
+  detail 0x7"). Seq is the number from the last EVENT_CONFIG reply for the
+  finger events and 0 for the frame event.
+- `39 ... ` LED_EX2, always 125 bytes, four fixed patterns (waiting for a
+  finger, off, success, failure), see tudor_led.h.
+- `80 <arg> 00 00 00 01 00 00 00 01 00 00 08 01 01 01 00` FRAME_ACQ, arg
+  0x0c for enroll and 0x14 for verify and identify, meaning unknown. `81`
+  FRAME_FINISH. Both reply with the status only.
+
+### One capture
+
+1. EVENT_CONFIG 0x06, wait for the notice (the sensor has an old event
+   pending whenever the last finger is still down or was just lifted, if
+   none comes the finger is up), EVENT_READ. If a finger down came without
+   a finger up: EVENT_CONFIG 0x04, wait, EVENT_READ until the finger up.
+   An EVENT_CONFIG off after every read.
+2. LED waiting, EVENT_CONFIG 0x01000000, FRAME_ACQ. The sensor takes the
+   image when a finger lands. Wait for the notice, EVENT_READ seq 0 for the
+   type 24 event.
+3. LED off, EVENT_CONFIG off, FRAME_FINISH.
+
+A rejected capture (flag) skips the processing step and starts over at 1.
+
+### Enroll (opcode 0x96)
+
+| command | meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `96 01` + 11 zeros | begin |
+| `96 02 00 00 00` | process the capture just taken |
+| `96 03 ...` | commit |
+| `96 04 00 00 00` | end |
+
+Reply of `96 02` (payload): template id (16, all zero until done), u32 60,
+u16 mask of captures taken (0x01, 0x03, 0x07 ... 0x7f), u16 percent
+(14 28 42 57 71 85 100), then counters and a quality of about 0x60. 7
+captures in the trace, and the id turns up in the reply of the 7th. After
+that: LED success, commit, end. The Windows driver also asks DB info
+between commit and end, not needed.
+
+Commit: `96 03`, 7 zero bytes, u32 length, then a list of type (u16),
+length (u32), data:
+
+| type | data |
+|---|---|
+| 0 | template id (16) |
+| 1 | identity: u32 2 (guid), 16 byte guid, zeros up to 76 bytes |
+| 2 | 1 byte finger, Windows numbering (1-5 right thumb..little, 6-10 left) |
+| 3 | payload, 17 bytes in Windows: the guid and the finger |
+
+The driver makes a new random guid per print so each print is a user of
+its own, and puts the libfprint user id string in the payload (cut at 64
+bytes, whether the sensor takes other sizes than 17 is untested).
+
+DB info after an enroll: users, templates and payloads all go up by one.
+
+### Identify (opcode 0x99)
+
+`99 01` + 11 zeros after a capture, the same for verify (the sensor has no
+verify). Status 0: match, payload is the template id (16) then fixed
+fields and, from offset 0x40 of the payload, the same type/length/data
+list as the commit (identity and payload of the match). Status 0x0905: no
+match, nothing else in the reply. LED success or failure after it.
+
+### Object info and delete
+
+- `a0 02 00 00 00 <template id>` object info of a template. Payload
+  offset 18 has the id of the user it belongs to (16 bytes), the bytes
+  before it are ff.
+- `a3 01 00 00 00 <user id>` deletes the user and everything below it.
+  Reply payload u16 3: the user, its template and its payload. DB info
+  afterwards: users, templates and payloads -1 each (counted as deleted).
+- The driver only deletes after `9f 02 00 00 00 <user id>` (templates of
+  that user, parent filter, not seen in the trace) returns exactly that
+  one template.
+
+### Storage and the print mapping
+
+The sensor only gives the payload back inside an identify reply, so the
+template to print mapping (username, finger) cannot be listed without a
+finger. Nothing in the trace reads an object's data (0xa1 in proto.txt).
+The driver treats the template id as the print: prints carry the id as
+data, verify and identify compare the id the sensor reports to the one in
+the print, list gives one print per template without username or finger.
+
 ## Safety
 
-tudor_cmd_allowed() lets through only GET_VERSION, PAIR, TLS_DATA and the
-two DB reads. The driver never sends bootloader patch, peek/poke,
-provision, ownership commands, storage writes, DB writes, request 0x15, or
-anything else not seen in the traces.
+tudor_cmd_allowed() lets through only commands seen in the traces: the
+ones for open and listing above, LED, events, frame acquire and finish,
+enroll 0x96 01..04, identify, object info on templates and the delete of a
+user. The driver never sends bootloader patch, peek/poke, provision,
+ownership commands, storage writes or formats, request 0x15, or anything
+else not seen in the traces.
 
 ## Unknowns
 
 - Meaning of request 0x19/0x1a (a probe the Windows driver always does),
   0x1b (session end).
 - Opcode 0xaf (`af 01 00 00 00`, 10 byte reply, after the status `0de2a15901ffffff`).
